A||||5||Homicidal||Waxing a white Fiero:  Any point?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-15-2001||06:38 PM||||As some of you know, I got an 84' SE, glossy white.  But when it comes to waxing, since it's a bright color, it's hard to see the shine and reflectivity.  I want to do it, but I want to see first if it makes a big difference in it's appearence.  Does anyone have any pics?  I know at night it would shine nicely though.....So, does anybody here wax their Whities?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Also, how much does a real nice high-gloss paintjob run?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-15-2001||07:03 PM||||Waxing a white Fiero is as important as waxing a red Fiero.  The wax protects the finish.  I'll grant you that it is hard to see the shine of a white Fiero in the brite sun but on a cloudy day or inside, the highly polished white Fiero glows.<P>The cost of a good paint job.  All panels removed and repainted, I've been quoted $3500.  Mr. Goodwrench repainted my 88 in 97 as a long term fill in project (over the winter)and it was $1300.  You can pay $120 at Maacco or $600 a Maacco.  Price will vary, quality will vary.||||reg||3
Z||000002||Cameron||04-15-2001||07:06 PM||||Personally I wouldn't wax a car just to make it more glossy.  I wax cars so that the paint on them is protected from the sun.  The glossy finish on them is just a by-product.  A good one I admit, but still.||||reg||3
Z||000003||johnt671||04-15-2001||07:21 PM||||I have a white 84 that wasn' waxed for a long time as the owner planned to kit car it. The paint on top is dead  and the trunk and hood had to be wet sanded and compounded, with the roof needing new paint.  As I'm not rich I've done this work by hand (afraid of a power buffer on my baby) and after four years may finish this spring.  Talk about a labor of love.  Long story short take care of what you got.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Frank Sombers||04-15-2001||08:13 PM||||Homicidal:<BR>I have a white '88 coupe and just polished and waxed it this weekend. Polished white cars don't just shine, they GLOW! Polishing and waxing (I recommend doing both) cleans your car, protects its finish, makes it look good, and also shows pride of ownership.<BR>Always remember "A Clean Car Has Class".<BR>Frank  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||04-15-2001||08:30 PM||||I owned a white Probe for about 4 years, and my father recently acquired a white 85 SE.  From experience, you can tell when the car has been waxed, although it might not be as noticeable as say a black car.  Even when you wash a car and dry it really good, once the wax thats on the paint wears off the dirt won't come off the finish well.  It simply won't shine up as well, even if its just dried water film on the car.  Kind of like bathroom tiles in the shower.  Its definately worth waxing for the shine IMO, but as has been stated, its even more important to protect the paint.  ||||reg||1
